1
00:00:01,970 --> 00:00:02,970
Hello, English learners.

2
00:00:03,360 --> 00:00:04,720
Welcome back to EnglishPod.

3
00:00:04,720 --> 00:00:05,230
My name is Marco.

4
00:00:05,670 --> 00:00:06,230
And I'm Erica.

5
00:00:06,850 --> 00:00:10,070
And today with Erica, we're bringing you a great lesson about time.

6
00:00:10,450 --> 00:00:11,190
You need more of it.

7
00:00:11,390 --> 00:00:12,490
We need more time, right?

8
00:00:12,880 --> 00:00:13,880
Yeah.

9
00:00:13,880 --> 00:00:16,440
So we're going to be teaching you how you can ask for more time.

10
00:00:16,440 --> 00:00:23,330
If you're working on a project at work and you can't finish it on time, you have to ask your boss for more time.

11
00:00:23,880 --> 00:00:24,880
Right.

12
00:00:24,880 --> 00:00:29,190
So in our dialogue, Casey needs more time to put together a press kit.

13
00:00:30,240 --> 00:00:30,990
What's a press kit?

14
00:00:31,430 --> 00:00:32,170
Good question.

15
00:00:32,450 --> 00:00:35,590
A press kit is something the marketing department will put together.

16
00:00:35,590 --> 00:00:41,010
Basically pictures, logos, information about the company.

17
00:00:41,680 --> 00:00:49,720
So when a newspaper or a magazine wants to write an article about them, so they have something already prepared that they could give the newspaper or magazine.

18
00:00:49,720 --> 00:00:50,010
Okay.

19
00:00:50,610 --> 00:00:53,290
So let's listen to our dialogue for the first time.

20
00:00:53,790 --> 00:00:58,070
And as always, it's going to be at a normal speed, so it might be a little bit difficult to understand.

21
00:00:58,520 --> 00:01:11,000
We'll come back in one minute and tell you about some of the key language.

22
00:01:11,000 --> 00:01:16,770
So Casey, how are things going with the photos for the press kit?

23
00:01:17,470 --> 00:01:20,710
Yeah, I've been meaning to talk to you about that.

24
00:01:21,490 --> 00:01:24,350
I might need to ask for an extension on that deadline.

25
00:01:25,330 --> 00:01:27,710
You've had over a month to get this finalized.

26
00:01:28,590 --> 00:01:29,990
Why are things delayed?

27
00:01:30,650 --> 00:01:35,250
Well, the thing is, we ran into a lot of problems.

28
00:01:35,710 --> 00:01:37,430
I'm not looking for excuses here.

29
00:01:37,830 --> 00:01:40,050
I just want to get this finished on time.

30
00:01:40,570 --> 00:01:41,070
I know.

31
00:01:41,390 --> 00:01:43,010
And I apologize for the delay.

32
00:01:43,560 --> 00:01:45,680
But some things were just beyond my control.

33
00:01:46,480 --> 00:01:47,980
I had trouble booking the photographer.

34
00:01:48,780 --> 00:01:50,740
And then Michael was sick for three weeks.

35
00:01:51,300 --> 00:01:52,680
So I couldn't include him in the photos.

36
00:01:53,580 --> 00:01:56,260
And the design team lost all the files.

37
00:01:56,660 --> 00:01:58,160
So I had to redo the pictures.

38
00:01:58,960 --> 00:02:01,180
I'm not going to put this off any longer, Casey.

39
00:02:01,840 --> 00:02:03,520
I want those photos ASAP.

40
00:02:08,650 --> 00:02:09,570
All right.

41
00:02:10,040 --> 00:02:12,310
So Casey is in trouble, I think.

42
00:02:13,010 --> 00:02:15,030
Yeah, I think her boss is pretty upset.

43
00:02:15,390 --> 00:02:15,530
Yeah.

44
00:02:16,760 --> 00:02:23,560
So let's take a look at some of the language that we saw in this dialogue in Language Takeaway.

45
00:02:23,560 --> 00:02:24,200
Language Takeaway.

46
00:02:25,460 --> 00:02:27,000
First word today is deadline.

47
00:02:29,260 --> 00:02:29,400
Deadline.

48
00:02:29,440 --> 00:02:30,440
Deadline.

49
00:02:30,440 --> 00:02:31,440
Deadline.

50
00:02:31,440 --> 00:02:32,320
So, Erica, what's a deadline?

51
00:02:33,360 --> 00:02:36,630
It is the last possible date to do something.

52
00:02:37,120 --> 00:02:41,470
So for example, when we were in school, our teacher would send us homework and say it was for Friday.

53
00:02:41,940 --> 00:02:43,110
So the deadline was Friday.

54
00:02:43,480 --> 00:02:44,430
Was Friday, right?

55
00:02:45,160 --> 00:02:48,650
Or when you're at work, you have projects that you have to finish by a certain date.

56
00:02:49,240 --> 00:02:49,770
That's your deadline.

57
00:02:50,240 --> 00:02:51,240
Exactly.

58
00:02:51,240 --> 00:02:52,310
Okay, let's look at our next word.

59
00:02:53,240 --> 00:02:54,240
Extension.

60
00:02:54,240 --> 00:02:55,240
Extension.

61
00:02:55,240 --> 00:02:56,240
Extension.

62
00:02:56,240 --> 00:02:57,240
Extension.

63
00:02:57,240 --> 00:03:00,920
So extension and deadline go together, right?

64
00:03:00,920 --> 00:03:01,920
Exactly.

65
00:03:01,920 --> 00:03:08,000
If you can't reach your deadline or you can't make your deadline, you must ask for an extension.

66
00:03:08,240 --> 00:03:08,560
An extension.

67
00:03:09,240 --> 00:03:10,620
So you ask for more time.

68
00:03:10,880 --> 00:03:11,000
Yeah.

69
00:03:11,720 --> 00:03:15,240
So extension is moving the deadline in the future.

70
00:03:15,240 --> 00:03:16,520
Alright, interesting.

71
00:03:16,520 --> 00:03:17,360
Let's look at our next word.

72
00:03:19,600 --> 00:03:20,320
Ran into.

73
00:03:20,320 --> 00:03:20,580
Ran into.

74
00:03:22,040 --> 00:03:22,540
Ran into.

75
00:03:23,280 --> 00:03:24,460
Ran into some problems.

76
00:03:25,200 --> 00:03:27,100
So we ran into some problems in this dialogue.

77
00:03:28,320 --> 00:03:32,120
Let's listen to some more examples of run into so we can understand the meaning.

78
00:03:33,400 --> 00:03:33,680
Example one.

79
00:03:36,280 --> 00:03:38,250
We ran into a storm on our way home.

80
00:03:41,840 --> 00:03:42,840
Example two.

81
00:03:45,160 --> 00:03:47,840
If you're not careful now, you're going to run into a lot of problems later.

82
00:03:51,800 --> 00:03:55,200
Example three.

83
00:03:55,200 --> 00:03:57,120
I ran into some trouble with my computer.

84
00:03:59,890 --> 00:04:00,970
Okay, good example.

85
00:04:01,360 --> 00:04:02,130
So it's clear now, right?

86
00:04:02,520 --> 00:04:03,520
Right.

87
00:04:03,520 --> 00:04:05,800
If you run into problems, you meet some problems.

88
00:04:05,800 --> 00:04:06,800
Right.

89
00:04:06,800 --> 00:04:07,730
Many students say it in that way.

90
00:04:08,000 --> 00:04:09,270
I met some problems.

91
00:04:09,610 --> 00:04:10,410
Yeah, but that's wrong.

92
00:04:10,670 --> 00:04:10,850
Right.

93
00:04:11,370 --> 00:04:13,570
So you would say I ran into some problems.

94
00:04:13,760 --> 00:04:14,760
Right.

95
00:04:14,760 --> 00:04:16,550
I met some problems is wrong, wrong, wrong.

96
00:04:17,200 --> 00:04:19,290
Okay, don't say met some problems.

97
00:04:19,470 --> 00:04:20,890
I ran into some problems.

98
00:04:20,990 --> 00:04:21,570
I ran into.

99
00:04:21,810 --> 00:04:22,130
Okay.

100
00:04:23,040 --> 00:04:25,350
And our last word for language takeaway, delayed.

101
00:04:26,040 --> 00:04:26,330
Delayed.

102
00:04:27,040 --> 00:04:27,590
Delayed.

103
00:04:28,110 --> 00:04:28,590
Delayed.

104
00:04:29,070 --> 00:04:30,970
So when something is delayed, it's late.

105
00:04:31,350 --> 00:04:31,570
Late.

106
00:04:32,130 --> 00:04:34,130
And the opposite is...

107
00:04:34,130 --> 00:04:34,550
On time.

108
00:04:35,370 --> 00:04:37,370
So your flight was delayed.

109
00:04:37,370 --> 00:04:38,730
Or your flight was on time.

110
00:04:38,910 --> 00:04:39,990
The project was delayed.

111
00:04:40,430 --> 00:04:42,150
Or your project was on time.

112
00:04:42,560 --> 00:04:43,560
Clear.

113
00:04:43,560 --> 00:04:44,560
Alright.

114
00:04:44,560 --> 00:04:45,560
So now let's go back to the dialogue.

115
00:04:46,000 --> 00:04:46,880
Let's listen to it again.

116
00:04:47,440 --> 00:04:52,200
And this time it will be a little bit slower so you can understand some of these phrases we just talked about.

117
00:05:01,000 --> 00:05:06,750
So Casey, how are things going with the photos for the press kit?

118
00:05:07,510 --> 00:05:10,810
Yeah, I've been meaning to talk to you about that.

119
00:05:11,570 --> 00:05:14,370
I might need to ask for an extension on that deadline.

120
00:05:15,190 --> 00:05:17,770
You've had over a month to get this finalized.

121
00:05:18,590 --> 00:05:20,090
Why are things delayed?

122
00:05:20,780 --> 00:05:25,320
Well, the thing is, we ran into a lot of problems.

123
00:05:25,760 --> 00:05:27,480
I'm not looking for excuses here.

124
00:05:27,900 --> 00:05:30,120
I just want to get this finished on time.

125
00:05:30,710 --> 00:05:31,470
I know.

126
00:05:32,070 --> 00:05:33,690
And I apologize for the delay.

127
00:05:34,510 --> 00:05:37,050
But some things were just beyond my control.

128
00:05:37,650 --> 00:05:39,410
I had trouble booking the photographer.

129
00:05:40,390 --> 00:05:44,230
And then Michael was sick for three weeks so I couldn't include him in the photos.

130
00:05:44,870 --> 00:05:50,090
And the design team lost all the files so I had to redo the pictures.

131
00:05:50,810 --> 00:05:53,970
I'm not going to put this off any longer, Casey.

132
00:05:54,630 --> 00:05:56,630
I want those photos ASAP.

133
00:06:03,950 --> 00:06:07,410
Okay, so there were some really useful phrases that we saw in this dialogue.

134
00:06:07,560 --> 00:06:12,720
Yeah, some wonderful phrases that you can use when you want to ask for more time.

135
00:06:12,720 --> 00:06:15,390
Okay, so let's look at these phrases in Fluency Builder.

136
00:06:17,080 --> 00:06:17,390
Fluency Builder.

137
00:06:18,230 --> 00:06:19,390
Alright, what's our first phrase?

138
00:06:20,270 --> 00:06:22,250
I've been meaning to talk to you.

139
00:06:22,960 --> 00:06:24,800
I've been meaning to.

140
00:06:24,800 --> 00:06:26,640
I've been meaning to.

141
00:06:26,680 --> 00:06:33,820
Okay, we have some really great examples of how you can combine I've been meaning to with different verbs.

142
00:06:34,200 --> 00:06:36,280
Yeah, let's listen.

143
00:06:36,280 --> 00:06:36,570
Example one.

144
00:06:39,080 --> 00:06:40,740
I'm sorry I haven't called you yet.

145
00:06:40,960 --> 00:06:42,700
I've been meaning to, but I've been busy.

146
00:06:46,100 --> 00:06:46,780
Example two.

147
00:06:50,320 --> 00:06:52,700
I've been meaning to tell you, but John quit yesterday.

148
00:06:56,770 --> 00:06:57,710
Example three.

149
00:07:00,990 --> 00:07:02,770
I've been meaning to see that film for a while.

150
00:07:05,840 --> 00:07:09,360
I've been meaning to go to that restaurant, but I haven't had time.

151
00:07:09,780 --> 00:07:12,480
So you've been wanting to for a long time.

152
00:07:12,720 --> 00:07:13,720
Right.

153
00:07:13,720 --> 00:07:14,880
I've been thinking about it.

154
00:07:15,020 --> 00:07:16,180
I've been planning to do it.

155
00:07:16,360 --> 00:07:17,140
But you just haven't done it.

156
00:07:17,240 --> 00:07:20,040
I haven't done it, maybe because I haven't had time or money.

157
00:07:20,800 --> 00:07:22,340
Okay, good phrase.

158
00:07:23,440 --> 00:07:26,770
Our next phrase is beyond my control.

159
00:07:28,080 --> 00:07:28,930
Beyond my control.

160
00:07:29,400 --> 00:07:30,630
It's beyond my control.

161
00:07:31,320 --> 00:07:33,430
So it means that I can't control it.

162
00:07:34,040 --> 00:07:35,130
You can't do anything about it.

163
00:07:35,240 --> 00:07:36,240
I can't do anything about it.

164
00:07:36,240 --> 00:07:37,850
The weather is beyond my control.

165
00:07:38,190 --> 00:07:42,970
Right, or relate with this project because Jane was sick for one month.

166
00:07:43,270 --> 00:07:44,350
It was beyond my control.

167
00:07:44,530 --> 00:07:45,830
Okay, it wasn't your fault.

168
00:07:46,030 --> 00:07:46,150
No.

169
00:07:47,360 --> 00:07:48,360
It was my fault.

170
00:07:48,360 --> 00:07:51,730
And our last phrase for fluency builder, put this off.

171
00:07:52,760 --> 00:07:53,650
Put this off.

172
00:07:55,080 --> 00:07:55,750
Put this off.

173
00:07:57,180 --> 00:07:59,200
Put this off.

174
00:07:59,200 --> 00:08:04,980
So let's listen to some more examples of put this off before we explain the meaning.

175
00:08:04,980 --> 00:08:08,240
Example one.

176
00:08:08,240 --> 00:08:11,760
I've been putting off this project for a week and it's due tomorrow.

177
00:08:15,880 --> 00:08:16,240
Example two.

178
00:08:17,640 --> 00:08:21,920
Can we put off our meeting until next week?

179
00:08:21,960 --> 00:08:23,980
I'm too busy right now.

180
00:08:26,380 --> 00:08:27,340
Example three.

181
00:08:30,080 --> 00:08:37,320
I've been putting off my email all day and now I have 50 messages to respond to.

182
00:08:37,320 --> 00:08:40,600
So Marco, when you put something off, you leave it for later.

183
00:08:41,240 --> 00:08:41,520
Okay.

184
00:08:42,240 --> 00:08:43,240
Right.

185
00:08:43,240 --> 00:08:47,340
So, maybe I have to write a report today, but I'm going to put it off till tomorrow.

186
00:08:47,360 --> 00:08:48,800
So you're going to do it tomorrow.

187
00:08:48,800 --> 00:08:49,400
I'll do it tomorrow.

188
00:08:49,880 --> 00:08:53,140
Okay, so we've seen some really great words and some really good phrases.

189
00:08:53,900 --> 00:08:58,220
So now let's listen to our dialogue for the last time and then we'll come back and talk some more.

190
00:09:04,150 --> 00:09:10,250
So Casey, how are things going with the photos for the press kit?

191
00:09:10,780 --> 00:09:14,180
Yeah, I've been meaning to talk to you about that.

192
00:09:15,000 --> 00:09:17,820
I might need to ask for an extension on that deadline.

193
00:09:18,740 --> 00:09:21,140
You've had over a month to get this finalized.

194
00:09:22,000 --> 00:09:23,420
Why are things delayed?

195
00:09:24,280 --> 00:09:28,740
Well, the thing is we ran into a lot of problems.

196
00:09:29,160 --> 00:09:30,920
I'm not looking for excuses here.

197
00:09:31,280 --> 00:09:33,500
I just want to get this finished on time.

198
00:09:34,080 --> 00:09:34,560
I know.

199
00:09:34,900 --> 00:09:39,150
And I apologize for the delay, but some things were just beyond my control.

200
00:09:40,030 --> 00:09:46,150
I had trouble booking the photographer and then Michael was sick for three weeks, so I couldn't include him in the photos.

201
00:09:47,070 --> 00:09:51,650
And the design team lost all the files, so I had to redo the pictures.

202
00:09:52,450 --> 00:09:54,670
I'm not going to put this off any longer, Casey.

203
00:09:55,320 --> 00:09:56,950
I want those photos ASAP.

204
00:09:58,320 --> 00:10:09,360
Well, Marco, do you think that Casey's reasons for being late are good?

205
00:10:09,360 --> 00:10:14,250
I think so, because it wasn't really her fault, the whole situation with the photographer.

206
00:10:15,240 --> 00:10:17,910
And then one of her co-workers was sick for three weeks.

207
00:10:18,920 --> 00:10:19,950
It's not really her fault, right?

208
00:10:20,160 --> 00:10:23,390
Right, but maybe the boss thinks that she's making excuses.

209
00:10:23,890 --> 00:10:25,210
Maybe she's making excuses.

210
00:10:25,930 --> 00:10:26,570
That's really common.

211
00:10:26,790 --> 00:10:31,100
Like, for example, when we were kids, you would say, oh, you know, my dog ate my homework.

212
00:10:31,500 --> 00:10:33,820
Right, but that really happened to me once.

213
00:10:34,640 --> 00:10:35,520
Your dog ate your homework?

214
00:10:35,600 --> 00:10:36,700
My cat ate my homework.

215
00:10:37,040 --> 00:10:38,140
Your cat ate your homework?

216
00:10:38,280 --> 00:10:39,280
Yeah.

217
00:10:39,280 --> 00:10:39,580
What did your teacher say?

218
00:10:40,280 --> 00:10:43,100
Well, actually, I was a really good student, so my teacher believed me.

219
00:10:44,000 --> 00:10:46,420
Well, my teacher wouldn't have believed me.

220
00:10:46,600 --> 00:10:46,760
Really?

221
00:10:47,020 --> 00:10:47,400
No, no.

222
00:10:47,660 --> 00:10:50,040
She would have called my parents, most likely.

223
00:10:50,680 --> 00:10:55,180
My cat really did eat my homework, and I just left it on the table, and it chewed it all up.

224
00:10:55,260 --> 00:10:55,680
That's weird.

225
00:10:57,040 --> 00:10:58,040
Why would your cat do that?

226
00:10:58,040 --> 00:10:59,040
I don't know.

227
00:10:59,040 --> 00:11:00,040
A lot of weird excuses out there.

228
00:11:00,040 --> 00:11:01,040
Yeah.

229
00:11:01,040 --> 00:11:02,640
I was abducted by aliens.

230
00:11:02,640 --> 00:11:05,680
My mom accidentally mailed my homework to Africa.

231
00:11:05,680 --> 00:11:05,970
Really?

232
00:11:06,680 --> 00:11:07,680
Yeah.

233
00:11:07,680 --> 00:11:08,680
This happened to you?

234
00:11:08,680 --> 00:11:09,680
Yeah.

235
00:11:09,680 --> 00:11:10,680
Writing, creative excuses.

236
00:11:11,470 --> 00:11:13,310
Well, I want to hear from our listeners.

237
00:11:13,850 --> 00:11:17,630
I want to know what excuses they've used for being late with something.

238
00:11:17,950 --> 00:11:20,750
So send us your excuses, send us your comments and questions.

239
00:11:21,270 --> 00:11:25,690
Go to our website at EnglishPod.com, where Erica and I are always there to answer your questions.

240
00:11:26,160 --> 00:11:28,560
That's right, Marco, and this is an elementary lesson, right?

241
00:11:28,840 --> 00:11:29,240
Exactly.

242
00:11:29,740 --> 00:11:37,560
But we also have intermediate and upper intermediate and advanced lessons for you more fluent English speakers.

243
00:11:37,820 --> 00:11:38,160
Exactly.

244
00:11:38,360 --> 00:11:43,600
So if you're listening to us on iTunes, be sure to visit our website and come see what else we have to offer.

245
00:11:43,980 --> 00:11:47,620
Well, I think we're out of time, but until next time, it's goodbye.
